    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Josh Naylor** (77.77) and **Brice Turang** (75.06) headlining our offensive attack. **Josh Naylor** is our premier play, benefiting from a massive 14% park factor boost and a favorable matchup against Hughes, whose 4.55 xERA provides the perfect runway for a high-output performance. Meanwhile, **Brice Turang** remains a tactical must-start; despite a slight platoon penalty, the aggressive 16% park adjustment and his position in the #2 hole\u2014combined with a specific focus on exploiting Povich\u2019s subpar holding capability\u2014make him an elite source of stolen base potential today. **Jose Caballero** also surges into must-start territory (73.66) due to an excellent 14% SP skill advantage and a \"green light\" environment against a slow-delivering pitcher, effectively neutralizing the inherent risk of his pending lineup status.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved **Austin Riley**, who narrowly missed the cut at a 54.26 score. While **Austin Riley** offers reliable power, the optimizer ultimately pivoted to **Vinnie Pasquantino** (54.34) and **Gabriel Moreno** (53.15) to maintain lineup balance, as **Austin Riley** suffered from a poor #8 batting order projection that capped his ceiling. Caution is advised regarding the bottom of our roster: **Moises Ballesteros** sits at a marginal 45.92 due to a stiff L/L platoon disadvantage against Connor Prielipp, and **Corbin Carroll** faces the toughest test of the day against Gerrit Cole. While **Corbin Carroll** remains in the lineup due to his elite sprint speed and favorable 85% SB opportunity score, he remains a high-variance asset against an elite arm. With **Giancarlo Stanton** confirmed on the IL, we are relying on our depth to carry the load, though any late-scratches for the pending lineups of **Moises Ballesteros** or **Jose Caballero** would necessitate an immediate re-evaluation of our utility slots.",
